{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789057918,"nonce":"KWSSuNyLgPrgypVFGoDk8C1y/WU=","message":"The value, at last. Instrumented the guard (std/src/fun.rs) to print what it saw; caught on run 11 of 120 with sqlite.wasm loaded:\n\n    Can't call closure whose type is not a function\n      (closure=0xc90790 type=0x80a464 kind=6 fun_ptr=0x0 hasValue=0)\n\nkind 6 is HF64. So the thing being called is a **boxed float**, not damaged memory: a vdynamic is {t, value} and a vclosure is {t, fun, hasValue, value}, so reading a boxed 0.0 as a closure gives exactly t=HF64, fun=0 (the double's low word), hasValue=0.\n\nThat rules out the corruption story I had been building. Nothing is clobbering the heap. A WRONG VALUE is reaching a closure slot.\n\nThe call site is utest's own code, and it is a plain iteration:\n\n    Dispatcher.dispatch:  var list = handlers.copy(); for (l in list) l(e);\n    Async.done:           resolved = true; timer.stop(); for (cb in callbacks) cb();\n\nSo an element of an Array\u003cVoid-\u003eVoid\u003e is a boxed float. The array is written only by utest, in Haxe, and the same code passes on interp, on AOT, and on wasm without a side module loaded.\n\nConditions, all three needed so far: the fiber transform, a side module loaded at run time, and luck (1 in 60 to 1 in 120 direct; every CI run).\n\nWhat that suggests, and what is NOT yet checked: a boxed 0.0 is what an EventLoop or Timer record's Float field looks like once boxed -- both hold a time beside a callback -- so reading the wrong field, or the right field of the wrong object, would produce this exactly. The next step is to find who writes that array element, not to keep looking at the reader.\n\nAlso worth checking, cheaply, before anything deeper: whether ANY side module triggers it or only sqlite (ui.wasm and sdl.wasm are built the same way), which separates 'side modules in general' from 'this library'.","files":null}]}